<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alor Setar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alor Star</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aror">Alor</a> Setar is the capital of the state of Kedah in north-western Peninsular Malaysia, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedah-river">Kedah River</a> amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> plains of the north. It became the royal seat of the sultans of Kedah in the 18th century and remains the home of the state's ruler. Its landmarks include the Zahir Mosque.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alor-setar.jpg" alt="Alor Setar" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sedorana2345 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlorSetarSkyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alor Setar lies in the north-west of Peninsular Malaysia, at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>, in the district of Kota Setar. Several rivers run around it, among them the Kedah, Langgar and Tajar. Mount Keriang, a limestone hill of 217.9 metres, rises nearby and contains a network of caves. The climate is tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with average highs around 32 °C and about 2,300 millimetres of rain a year. The name joins two Malay words, alor, a small stream, and setar, a tree related to the mango, Bouea macrophylla. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> growing was traditionally the basis of the local economy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was established in the 18th century by a sultan of Kedah as the eighth administrative centre of the sultanate since its founding in 1136. The site was a small village, which the sultan found well placed at the point where the two estuaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedah-river">Kedah River</a> meet. With the agreement of several nobles it was founded as Kota Setar, the Town of Setar. The sultan lived in the Istana Kota Setar.</p><p>In 1770 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> fleet under Raja Haji destroyed the palace and the Balai Besar, the Grand Hall. In 1771 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-light">Francis Light</a> for the British and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-mukarram-shah">Abdullah Mukarram Shah</a> made an agreement to halt Bugis influence in Kedah. On 17 March 1805, during the coronation of Ahmad Tajuddin Halim Shah II, Siamese forces occupied the capital, and the sultan withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. The town stood still for twenty years. He was restored in 1842, and the capital moved briefly to Kota Kuala Muda before returning to Kota Setar. The railway station opened in 1915 and the airport in 1929, among the early ones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alor Setar is the state capital of Kedah and the seat of the Central State Administration Centre, and the sultan's official residence is at Anak Bukit. It is the second-largest city in the state, after Sungai Petani. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> population was 374,051 in 2020. The name was changed to Alor Star at a ceremony proclaiming city status on 21 December 2003 and the old name was restored on 15 January 2009. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a>, the first prime minister of Malaysia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahathir-mohamad">Mahathir Mohamad</a> were born there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alor Setar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultans later moved their seat to <b>Alor Setar</b> on the rice plains of the Kedah River.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
